{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the politics of the region.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born in 1140. His father Raymond II was killed in 1152 and Raymond III succeeded him as Count of Tripoli at the age of 12. The regency was initially held by his mother Hodierna but she died in 1152. The regency was then transferred to the constable of the county William Dorel. Raymond III came of age in 1155 and took control of the county.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1174. She was the daughter of William of Bures Prince of Galilee. The marriage was likely arranged by King Amalric I of Jerusalem who was Raymond III's cousin. The marriage strengthened the ties between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Imprisonment*\nIn 1187 Raymond III joined the army of King Guy of Lusignan to face the forces of Saladin at the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and Raymond III was taken prisoner by Saladin. He was imprisoned in Damascus for several years.\n\n*Release and Later Life*\nRaymond III was released from prison in 1193. He returned to the County of Tripoli and resumed his rule. However his health had deteriorated significantly during his imprisonment and he died in 1187. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III was a significant figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played an important role in the politics of the region. His reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim forces and his eventual imprisonment and release. Despite his challenges he managed to maintain the integrity of the County of Tripoli and ensure its survival."